        "id": "ch2_7-1",
        "text": "Chapter 2 I Bash Scripting\n57\nThis Bash script (in Figure 2.3) is divided into three sections:\nI\nI In the first section, we create all the global variables. Global variables are\naccessible inside any function you create. For example, we are able to use\nall the NUM variables declared in the example inside the add function.\nI\nI Next, we build the functions by dividing our applications into logical\nsections. The print_custom() function will just print any text that we\ngive it. We\u2019re using the $1 to access the parameter value passed to this\nfunction (which is the string CALCULATOR ).\nI\nI Finally, we call each function sequentially (each one by its name). Print\nthe header, add the numbers, and, finally, print the results.\nConditions and Loops\nNow that you know the basics of Bash scripting, we can introduce more advanced\ntechniques. When you develop programs in most programming languages (e.g.,\nPHP, Python, C, C++, C#, etc.), including Bash scripting, you will encounter\nconditions ( if statements) and loops, as shown in Figure 2.4.\nFigure 2.3: Script Sections\n58\nChapter 2 I Bash Scripting\nEsta secci\u00f3n contiene las siguientes subsecciones\n\u2022 Conditions\n\u2022 Loops\nSi deseas explorar cualquiera de estas subsecciones, puedes pregunt\u00e1rmelo directamente.",
